raye-update (0.2~beta1) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Updates installed at restart need one restart, not two, unless the
    kernel or the boot chain changed.
  * The update screen keeps the system language.
  * Updating raye-update itself no longer stops the install it is part of.
  * Notices say "1 update" and "4 updates", not "update(s)", in both
    languages.
  * A Raye archive that is not published yet no longer throws away Ubuntu's
    updates.

 -- techoraye <190692880+techoraye@users.noreply.github.com>  Sun, 08 Nov 2026 11:07:43 +0000

raye-update (0.1~dev3) resolute; urgency=medium

  * A place updates come from that cannot be read no longer stops the
    others. Measured on apt 3.0.3: a name that does not resolve is a
    warning and apt exits 0, while a missing index or a signature that
    does not check is an error and apt exits 100 -- so a 404 on our own
    archive used to stop every Ubuntu security update with it, and a PC
    that reached nothing at all was told it was up to date. The list of
    updates now comes from the simulation, which works on the indexes apt
    does have; the repositories that failed are named in brokenSources,
    with why; and when every one of them failed we say so instead of
    claiming freshness.

